WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.220
Film Courage: How did you know you were rushing
through your first film, was it feedback was

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.370
it an epiphany you had?

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:07.820
Zeke Zelker, Writer/Director/Producer of the
transmedia project BILLBOARD: Just me watching

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:12.519
my own material. I think that filmmakers need
to be honest with themselves. And not everything

00:00:12.519 --> 00:00:17.119
you make is going to be great but everything
you make should be a testing ground for your

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:18.140
next project.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:23.599
We are filmmakers, it’s a craft. You have
to remember we are craftsmen. We are crafts

00:00:23.599 --> 00:00:28.230
ladies you know. And so it’s a matter of
with film you bring all of those craftspeople

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:35.250
together to create one piece of work. It’s
one of those things that people don’t think

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.870
about enough and you also hear these talks
to students and they say “Listen you’ll

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:44.510
learn more by doing then you will in school.”
Because you have to do it. You have to fail.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.609
You have to figure out what works and doesn’t
work.

00:00:46.609 --> 00:00:52.460
But I learned that I was rushing through things
by watching stuff myself after the fact. I

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:58.219
was a bit naive, a bit arrogant in think that
what I would make would make money and thankfully

00:00:58.219 --> 00:00:59.600
it all has.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:05.920
It’s interesting I have a different business
model (I keep saying that). All of my films

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:11.810
have been profitable except for one. It’s
the only film that we sold. It was very strange

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:19.020
because I firmly believe in theatrical distribution.
I believe in what I call a $10,000 screening

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.640
where you are actually netting $10,000 at
a screening.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:28.230
So I will rent the largest place possible,
we’ll sell as many tickets as we can, we

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:37.340
tier the ticket prices - $10.00, $35.00, $125.00
(that’s one hundred twenty-five dollars).

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:42.259
For instance $10.00 just gets you in the film.
$35.00 is the film and after party. The $125.00

00:01:42.259 --> 00:01:49.220
is a pre-party, after party and then “swag
bag” types of stuff. Then we hand out film

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:54.710
bills that we sell advertising in. And then
we have merch and all of these types of things.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:02:02.430
I literally net $10,000 per screening. And
I generally do that in the early parts of

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:10.280
film release and I’ve been successful at
it. So you string enough together and you

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:11.640
are in the black.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.700
It’s just a model that I’ve created for
myself. I’ve always had some other type

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:20.030
of IP [intellectual property] involved. Like
for instance with AKA (the film I told you

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.490
about earlier), the film tanked, it didn’t
do well. I made more money on a cocktail book

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:29.570
then I did associated with the project then
I did with the project itself. But I still

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:33.270
equate that with the film itself as it’s
all part of the same property. So I always

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:36.460
think about creating different revenue streams
for every project.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:43.100
For instance with BILLBOARD I created a radio
station WTYT 960. We have the web series,

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:48.110
we have not the interactive play, we have
the feature film and then with all that stuff

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:55.310
we can sell sponsorships because we are actually
showing off brands within the content that

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:59.370
we’ve created. So that drives a whole other
level of income.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:05.820
Film Courage: How many sponsorships do you
have for BILLBOARD?

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:17.210
Zeke: Four…six…seven…eight…there’s
nine, nine sponsors and I’ll be very candid

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:21.960
they go anywhere from $9,000 up to $40,000.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:27.550
Film Courage: I see…so $9,000 is your minimum
entry point?

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:28.550
Zeke: Yes.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:34.100
Film Courage: And do you know these…that’s
fantastic. Did you have prior business relationships

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:35.721
with any of the sponsors or…

00:03:35.721 --> 00:03:36.721
Zeke: No.

00:03:36.721 --> 00:03:37.721
Film Courage: Or you just cold-called…

00:03:37.721 --> 00:03:42.120
Zeke: Cold-called. But I also firmly believe
that filmmakers need to build audience early

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:48.031
on. And so what we did first is we created
the radio station WTYT 960 where now there

00:03:48.031 --> 00:03:54.220
is well over a 1,000 bands on it. I think
we are in 22 countries, 49 states. And so

00:03:54.220 --> 00:04:01.520
I did that because I knew that we had an audience.
And no sponsor is going to give you anything

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.270
you can prove that you’re going to have
impression counts and they know that they

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:07.950
are going to have some sort of audience for
their project.

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:14.740
So no only do we have those 1,000 bands but
we have fans. And we did an average of them.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:22.040
Each of the bands average $6,000 fans. So
all of the sudden 1,000 bands and 6,000 fans

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:28.270
you are at what…6 million reach? Which is
pretty astronomical for a small independent

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:33.620
film. So we were able to get those numbers
because we could prove that we actually had

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:38.000
those impressions. And so we had audience
and so that’s what’s fascinating about

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.210
all of that because it’s not easy to do
but we were able to do it.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:45.700
Film Courage: With the film screenings you
said you netted $10,000?

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:46.750
Zeke: 10 grand.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:49.420
Film Courage: What’s your split with the
theater? How are you doing this?

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:50.420
Zeke: I rent them.

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:52.450
Film Courage: So you buy it out?

00:04:52.450 --> 00:05:01.390
Zeke: So mind you it’s not every screening
we do $10,000. Early on we found that we do

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:06.610
better when we have events around our projects
rather just running for a week or something

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:07.690
like that.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:13.760
So we are going to be reformulating that model
a little bit and taking it on the road. But

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:19.830
what we do is we launch the film by that $10,000
screening because also too that frees up capital

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:25.520
and also builds buzz that we also have a very
good box office. And that’s how it’s all

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:26.520
generated.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.980
I’ve written about this a couple of times.
Like I’ve written for something in Ted Hope’s

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:36.640
blog about the $10,000 screening and things.
It’s very possible. Every filmmaker can

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:43.740
do it, they really can. The interesting thing
is that because I create so far out of the

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:49.420
system in New York and Los Angeles that there
is anomalies that&nbsp;&nbsp;happen because of that.

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:53.250
Like I don’t know if it can happen in LA
quite honestly? Because there are so many

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:57.660
filmmakers, so many films here that I don’t
know if it would work? Nor in New York but

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.380
it does work where I’m from.

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:04.010
Film Courage: Yes…I was just going to ask
you…do you have such a leg up because it’s

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:07.610
a smaller area, they want to support local
artists, they are excited, maybe they are

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:08.660
even in the film?

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:15.790
Zeke: Yes, absolutely…to a point but at
the same time it has its plusses and minuses.

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:21.500
It’s harder for me to produce in my hometown
because of that because I have to bring in

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:22.500
everybody.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:26.630
There is colleges that have film programs
and I end up teaching everybody and then once

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:32.020
I teach them they stick around for a couple
of years and come to LA because I don’t

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:37.890
produce enough fast enough to be able to sustain
my people over the course of a long period

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:38.890
of time.

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:44.840
So we end up bringing in people from New York
and Philadelphia for crew and we also fly

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:48.840
in cast and crew from LA. So that’s a problem,
it’s like when you are producing in places

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:55.840
that are more remote they do cost more. People
don’t think about that, catering, per diem,

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:57.130
lodging, transportation.

00:06:57.130 --> 00:07:02.070
And then people are like well you get tax
incentives and yada yada yada. Well you only

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:06.620
get them if you qualify for them. So there
are certain things you have to be aware of

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:09.380
and again it comes down to the business side
of things.

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:13.510
Like for instance I produce all of the cashflow.
Not a lot of filmmakers do that so I don’t

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:21.250
qualify for a lot of tax incentives because
I don’t have the $800,000 in the bank after

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:27.790
July 1st, after they passed their budget.
I basically make money and spend money, that’s

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:33.620
my business model. And so I don’t qualify.

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:41.660
Like for instance producing outside of New
York and LA and Atlanta. Filmmakers just need

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:46.680
to know what their state tax incentive is
before they even start to think about budgeting.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:49.280
It’s also where are they getting their capital
from?

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:57.680
There are also different tricks to be able
to obtain capital. I’m horrible at crowdfunding.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:02.260
I have a hard time asking people for money.
I think that’s one reason why I have very

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:09.400
few equity partners. I generally spend my
own money (my wife and my money quite frankly).

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:23.240
Like we are like 82% of the resources that
it took to make BILLBOARD.

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:26.800
But I believe in being all in. I believe I
need to have skin in the game. So I’m horrible

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:29.650
for asking them for money…horrible. I need
another producer to ask for money. I can make

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:33.701
the money trust me. I can make the money.
I just have a horrible time asking people

00:08:33.701 --> 00:08:34.701
for money.

00:08:34.701 --> 00:08:41.990
Film Courage: When then you may feel a freedom
too that this is literally mine (our) project.

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:47.950
If it’s 80 percent your money there is less
need to get approval from other people for

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:48.950
things.

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:52.920
Zeke: You’re absolutely right. But at the
same time there is a lot more risk. There’s

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:56.630
a lot more sleepless nights. I’m worried
about making payroll.

00:08:56.630 --> 00:09:02.330
Literally the people who watch the film, the
people who work with me have said it has become

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:03.330
autobiographical.

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:08.700
It’s interesting because we’ve been screening
the film which much success thank goodness.

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:12.480
And some people have pushed back because they
don’t like the ending and things. And I’m

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:20.610
like “I understand that.” And they will
start arguing with me in the Q&amp;A which doesn’t

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:25.450
make any sense to me but like I will literally
ask them a simple question

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:27.360
“Have you ever owned a business?”

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:28.360
“Well…um.”

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:33.490
“No! Have you ever owned a business? Have
you ever had to make payroll?”

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:35.730
“Well my father did.”

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.320
“Well did you?”

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:40.730
“Well my father lost his business.”

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:42.760
I’m like “Exactly, exactly.”

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:47.460
And so the thing is people can’t really
walk the walk until they’ve talked the talk.

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:51.740
Wait did I turn that around? No…talk the
talk unless they’ve walked the walk. And

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:58.020
so my wife and I we literally put everything
on the line. It’s scary as hell. It’s

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:05.730
risky as hell. But I’ve made money in the
past so I keep doing it. Thank goodness we

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:06.730
have been successful.

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:12.080
Also too people can’t look at filmmaking
as a lottery. I have so many friends and peers

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:16.890
that see getting into Sundance as a lottery
ticket like they are going to have this huge

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:19.830
and career and everything else and that sort
of thing.

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:24.140
I love Sundance. I’ve had films in Sundance.
That’s not what I’m saying at all. But

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:27.890
people have to look at the long tail of their
careers. That everything is a building block

00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:32.480
and you don’t want to be a flash in the
pan because there are only a few of them.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:37.860
You can probably name them on your two hands
right? How many films have been produced over

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:44.121
the past 10 years that have gone nowhere.
People have to think about it from the a business

00:10:44.121 --> 00:10:51.240
perspective and realizing how they can make
money at it early on and it’s just a matter

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:55.210
of looking at it like “Oh my gosh I’m
going to make this movie. I’m going to be

00:10:55.210 --> 00:11:00.290
rich.” But looking at it as “I’m making
the movie. This is part of my business.”

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:05.740
If you make widgets, you might make widgets
over the course of your factory’s career.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:10.680
If you’re a filmmaker you are going to make
different films over the course of your career.

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:17.380
It’s not all dependent on one film. So just
think about it that way. And I’m saying

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:24.710
that I’ve been hugely successful but I’ve
made money and I do this full time. But it’s

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:29.690
a grind. I work my butt off still to this
day and I lead by example. I'm the first one

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:33.899
in my studio by 7:00 a.m. and I'm the last
one to leave at 6:30 - 7:00 p.m. I've been

00:11:33.899 --> 00:11:37.930
doing that for 10 years working on the same
project.

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:43.280
It's a matter of vision, it's a matter of
just really working and yes it's 6 and a half

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:43.910
days a week.

